@NODE Background "What: Background and Purpose"
[------------( Background and Purpose )-----------------------------------]

Upload Activities is a door for amiexpress intended for MULTINODE systems
only and can't be used on single node systems. The main purpouse of this
door is to show if a user on any other node is uploading something, and if
so, it tells you what's in his batch queue and what files in his batch have
already been finished uploading.

NOTE: Take a look at the included output example (What.Output)

WHAT can be made resident. I really recommend you to do this, since
it will speed this door up quite much, specially on 68000-68020 computers!
@ENDNODE
@NODE Features "What: Notable Features"
[------------( Notable Features )-----------------------------------------]

Several reasons to use Upload Activities:

* Written entirely in C for fast code.
* Can be made resident.
* First kind of this door for amiexpress.
* Configable playpen directories.
* Special optimized versions for all CPU types.
* 32-Node compatable. (Actually how many nodes you want)
* Using Icons (/X3.x Standard Nowadays), but should work fine with /X2.x
  and Sigma Express.
@ENDNODE
@NODE Install "What: Install into Express"
[------------( Install into Express )-------------------------------------]

This door should be installed as a standard amiexpress door.
On /X3.x you have you have to edit (or create) a file called WHAT.INFO in
your BBSCmd directory. On /X2.x and SExpress you have to edit the
BBS.Cmd in the usual way. Remember to give it a high stack (example 50000)
and make it multinode. The door is an XIM type ofcourse!

Also, for added speed, you may make the door resident with your normal
"resident" command (CLI: "Resident What.x PURE"). But if you do, make
sure that Express runs just the doorfilename, without its path. On /X3.x
you also need the tooltype RESIDENT in the icon.

You also need a icon in doors:. Just create a info file named WHAT.INFO.
Make it as a drawer icon to your DOORS:WHAT dir. Here you can put any
of the available tooltypes listed later in this document.
@ENDNODE
@NODE Tooltypes "What: Available Tooltypes"
[------------( Available Tooltypes )--------------------------------------]

Here are the available tooltypes.

Tooltype-Name:                    Description:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODES=<#nodes>                  - This tooltype is really not necessarly.
                                  This is how many nodes you have. (Or how
                                  many nodes you want to search through).
                                  If this tooltype is not found, it will
                                  search through all 32 nodes.

DONOTSHOW_NODE<node>            - This will skip the search for upload on
                                  the specified node.

PLAYPEN_<node>                  - This is ONLY needed if you have external
                                  playpen directories.
@ENDNODE
